q. v.] Primarily, a sort of scepter or staff of office, the attribute of Folly personified, carried by the jesters of kings and great lords in the middle ages, and down to the seventeenth century. {{smaller|It is generally represented as crowned with the head of a fool or zany, wearing a party-colored hood with asses’ ears, and with a ring of little bells, like sleigh-bells. At the other end there was sometimes a ball or bladder inflated with air, with which to belabor people. Also spelled ''bawble''.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The kynges foole
Sate by the fire upon a stoole,
As he that with his ''bauble'' plaide.</poem>
{{right|''Gower'', Conf. Amant., vii.}}}}
{{smaller block|Fools, who only wanted a party-coloured coat, a cap, and a ''bawble'', to pass for such amongst reasonable men.
{{right|''Dryden'', Post. to Hist. of League.}}}}
