'''baublery†''', ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''bablerie'', ''babelry''; < ''bauble''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ry''.] Childish trifling; a trifling matter.

'''baubling†''' (bâ′bling), ''a.'' [< ''bauble''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', + ''-ing''<sup>2</sup>.] Contemptible; paltry.
{{smaller block|A ''baubling'' vessel was he captain of. ''Shak.'', T. N., v. 1.}}

'''bauch, baugh''' (bäċh), ''a.'' [Sc., perhaps < Icel. ''bāgr'', uneasy, poor, hard up; cf. ''bagr'', awkward, clumsy.] Weak; pithless; shaky. [Scotch.]

'''bauchee-seed''' (bâ′chē-sēd), ''n.'' Same as ''bawchan-seed''.

